import{_ as n,P as s,d as o,e as r,x as a,q as i,f as l}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging","xkChars":2,"xkWords":3956,"xkLessons":{"C.7":{"title":"Sufficient information and correct execution are separate","lead":"A decoder cannot recover a lost distinction; an adequate representation can still be used incorrectly.","explanation":["If q=g∘f, a second conversion h preserves the same final query only when g is constant on the fibers of h restricted to the actual reachable set f(X). Checking every imaginable intermediate value is unnecessary; preserving the distinctions relevant to the final answer on the actual image is essential.","The first conversion can correctly retain a pair of bits while a later sum correctly counts them, yet their composition fails if the final query still asks for the first bit. Changing the final query to the count creates a different successful task rather than repairing the original one.","Actual execution adds another check. If two source states share a representation but need different answers, there is representational insufficiency. If a third representation unambiguously requires one answer but the actual rule returns another, there is also an independent rule error. Fixing the latter does not remove the former."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Representation insufficiency","leftText":"The retained value merges states requiring different target answers.","rightTitle":"Execution error","rightText":"The actual rule returns a wrong answer despite the relevant retained distinction."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Keeping two bits, then only their sum","text":"The states (0,1) and (1,0) have the same sum but different first bits. The sum answers a counting query but cannot recover the first bit. Keeping that bit separately repairs the specified query.","assumptions":"Finite deterministic bit pairs; final query remains the first bit.","boundary":"No general claim that every compression fails or all details must be retained."}],"boundaries":["A real fault may remain unclassified when its actual input or execution rule is unknown.","Do not erase locally correct outputs because other states fail."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If an original document existed somewhere, was the decision maker’s input sufficient?","answer":"Not necessarily. The document must have been accessible in time to the actual actor and rule. Later reconstructability is not timely availability.","basis":"Theory v2, §C.7, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[{"title":"Mars Climate Orbiter: the unit mapping","href":"/en/cases/mars-climate-orbiter"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.7","startLine":4609,"endLine":4618}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/appendix-c#section-c-7","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§C.7; English reading edition, lines 4609–4618","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}]},"C.9":{"title":"The right extra variable depends on the task","lead":"A mean can preserve total load while losing local overload and position.","explanation":["For two ordered queues with lengths from 0 to 30, the source defines local overload as a maximum above 15. States (0,20) and (10,10) share mean 10 but require opposite overload answers. The mean is correct; its information is insufficient for that target.","Adding the maximum recovers overload and maximum length. With two queues, identical per-position service capacity c, and no new tasks, the smaller queue is 2m−M, so the pair (mean, maximum) also closes under the specified symmetric update.","The result changes with the task and dynamics. Different capacities tied to position make the location of the large queue relevant. With three queues, mean and maximum need not determine the updated total. The finite uniform-state error count is exact for the constructed domain, not an estimate of a real queue population."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Accurate average","leftText":"Correctly describes the declared mean and, with queue count, total.","rightTitle":"Task sufficiency","rightText":"Retains all distinctions needed for the particular answer or update."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"A location-specific service change","text":"With capacities (15,10), states (0,20) and (20,0) share mean 10 and maximum 20. They update to (0,10) and (5,0), so their next mean and maximum differ.","assumptions":"Two ordered queues, no arrivals, each served up to its assigned capacity.","boundary":"The example establishes failure of this representation under this update, not universal failure of aggregation."}],"boundaries":["The 961 equally weighted states are a teaching distribution, not measured frequencies.","Do not attribute a poor decoding rule’s avoidable errors entirely to representation loss."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Must every micro-level detail be restored to detect overload?","answer":"No. The maximum suffices for this target. A different target or operation may require a different retained distinction.","basis":"Theory v2, §C.9, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[{"title":"Representation and local overload","href":"/en/concepts/representation"},{"title":"Hurricane Ian: a different target needs different information","href":"/en/cases/representation-hurricane-cone"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.9","startLine":4631,"endLine":4646}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/appendix-c#section-c-9","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§C.9; English reading edition, lines 4631–4646","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}]},"C.12":{"title":"Three forms of composition need three different checks","lead":"Preserving an answer, composing a causal effect, and executing tasks together are not one “connected” status.","explanation":["Function composition asks whether successive representations preserve the same final query. Causal composition asks whether joint responses and conditions support an effect through the whole chain. Resource composition asks whether the combined tasks satisfy shared constraints. The same workflow can contain all three problems.","The source’s two-task example isolates resources and deadlines. Each task occupies one full slot, both are due in period one, and that period has capacity one. Each is feasible alone; the pair is not. Future capacity cannot repair the original missed deadline unless the deadline is validly changed.","F3 adds an information constraint: one observation may leave several compatible states. A single acceptable action exists precisely when their acceptable-action sets have a nonempty intersection. Each state having some good action is insufficient. Those sets must already include the task’s actual resource, protection, and valid-permission requirements."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Each state or task has a solution","leftText":"Separate feasible sets are nonempty.","rightTitle":"A common feasible action or plan","rightText":"The relevant intersection or combined constraint set is nonempty."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Opposite actions under the same observation","text":"If one compatible state allows only left and another only right, both are individually solvable but no one action works in both. A valid preservation action allowed in both would make the intersection nonempty.","assumptions":"Finite, single-round, deterministic task and explicitly listed compatible states.","boundary":"Unknown states, sequential strategies, and risk-tolerant decisions require their own analysis."}],"boundaries":["A formal feasibility proof does not certify real resources or permissions.","Legal observation, waiting, or preservation may change the available strategy set, but cannot be assumed costless or automatically available."],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can two independently available permissions be combined to authorize an action neither covers?","answer":"No. The mathematical action set cannot manufacture valid authority; permissions must be applicable at the relevant nodes under their governing rules.","basis":"Theory v2, §C.12, with the distinctions explained above."}],"caseLinks":[{"title":"Joint feasibility","href":"/en/concepts/joint-feasibility"},{"title":"Causal chains","href":"/en/concepts/chain"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"C.12","startLine":4686,"endLine":4701}],"sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2-en","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation — English reference edition","url":"/en/theory/appendix-c#section-c-12","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Theory v2, November 2026","locator":"§C.12; English reading edition, lines 4686–4701","claim_support":["The section’s definitions, argument, and conditions, interpreted in this guide."],"limits":["The Chinese original remains authoritative. This is a theory source, not independent empirical support for a real case."]}],"model":"joint"}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-c.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-c.md"}'),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-c.md"};function h(d,e,p,u,m,f){const t=s("SectionLesson");return o(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a('<div class="theory-source"><p><a id="part_19"></a></p><h1 id="part-title-19" tabindex="-1">Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-title-19" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging {#part-title-19}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This appendix lists methodological responsibilities that can be shared when texts interoperate. Each text retains its own authoritative definitions and way of operating. The common kernel requires no shared chapter structure, artifact count, or numbering. It does not make one text independent empirical evidence for another.</p><h2 id="section-c-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Method Kernel and Stable References <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.1 Method Kernel and Stable References {#section-c-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Chinese Reference</th><th>Content</th><th>Where to Find It</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition layer</td><td>Objects, states, feedback, scales, circles, information status, and recasting</td><td>Framework definitions and authoritative definitions in <em>World Model</em></td></tr><tr><td>Method constraints CMC1–CMC3</td><td>Task and representation, evidential status of materials, and facts and eligibility to act</td><td>§4.17</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional formal propositions F</td><td>Task recovery, closure, common feasible actions, composition of conversions, error, and dynamic refinement</td><td>§4.18–4.19 and this appendix</td></tr><tr><td>Empirical question families G1–G4</td><td>Grouping, channels, historical increments, and representational closure</td><td>Instance contracts in Part 4</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome and mechanism contracts C1–C12</td><td>Outcome registration, mechanism composition, and bridging to normative procedures</td><td>The individual contracts in Part 4</td></tr><tr><td>Governance invariants</td><td>Freezing, evidence, baselines, authorization, protection, and the life cycle</td><td>Existing governance clauses in both texts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="section-c-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Common Method Constraints <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.2 Common Method Constraints {#section-c-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>CMC1 requires the task, representation, horizon, environment, operations, and error to be declared together. Test static recovery, dynamic closure, and intervention sufficiency separately. CMC2 requires preserving the evidential status of materials, source lineage, inferential premises, and scope of support. Reprocessing material does not count as independent empirical evidence. CMC3 requires facts, value judgments, and eligibility to act to be established separately. See §4.17 for the full formulation.</p><p>The three constraints specify responsibilities for using models. They do not constitute three empirical laws of the world. Whether they reduce errors or impose unnecessary burdens in real tasks requires separate evaluation. Compliance with the procedures does not establish the framework’s effectiveness.</p><h2 id="section-c-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Shared Governance Invariants <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.3 Shared Governance Invariants {#section-c-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Invariant one. Freeze the record before results and only append afterward.</p><p>Invariant two. Lack of support for a positive claim does not amount to support for the null model.</p><p>Invariant three. Definitions, contracts, internal teaching examples, and internal simulations cannot supply external empirical support on their own.</p><p>Invariant four. The evidential status of materials, proposition role, artifact status, and whether fields are missing are orthogonal dimensions. Keep them separate.</p><p>Invariant five. A single artifact cannot automatically satisfy responsibilities for source tracing, modeling, foresight, evaluation, authorization, and execution.</p><p>Invariant six. Model complexity must be subject to simplification, removal of unnecessary elements, and review of necessity suited to the task. Claims of predictive or empirical capability require comparison with a simple baseline and independent evaluation material. Formal proofs, interpretation of texts, and normative arguments are checked according to their own tasks. Inapplicable out-of-sample metrics must not be imposed on them.</p><p>Invariant seven. Residuals generate only competing candidates. They do not automatically generate variables in reality.</p><p>Invariant eight. Capabilities have domains of applicability and validity periods, and are subject to drift, suspension, rollback, and retirement.</p><p>Invariant nine. Aggregate results must not erase low-power positions, privacy, non-disclosure for protection, appeal, or exit.</p><h2 id="section-c-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Preserving Differences Across Texts <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.4 Preserving Differences Across Texts {#section-c-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Interoperability should separately check proposition layers, empirical question-family contracts, null conclusions, scale closure, noncommutation tests, and inferential dependencies, as well as information status, artifact systems, typed unknowns, experimental evaluation, evidence of capability, and model life cycles. A complete contract in one text cannot be replaced by a similar name or case in the other. When the correspondence has yet to be established, keep the differences explicit.</p><p>The framework’s nine scale operators need not be aligned with the standard operator steps in <em>World Model</em>. The framework’s 14-section scale transformation record is also kept separate from the closed artifact system of <em>World Model</em>. The two share only the minimum meanings of what is preserved, changed, collapsed, or omitted, new unknowns, source lineage, domain of applicability, residuals, and stopping.</p><h2 id="section-c-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Mathematical Example of Bridging Scales <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.5 A Mathematical Example of Bridging Scales {#section-c-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Consider a microstate x, a coarse-graining map M, microscopic evolution F, and macroscopic evolution F̄. If the difference between M(F_t(x)) and F̄_t(M(x)) remains within tolerance ε over the specified initial conditions, parameter domain, error norm, and time interval, one can discuss approximate commutation for that task. If this holds for only one statistic, it cannot be extended to equivalence of the entire state. A proof must also state the order of limits, how the error is controlled, and the interval of validity. A short-time approximation alone cannot establish a long-term conclusion. This is a general formal example and does not claim to give such a theorem for social objects.</p><p>Mathematical bridging requires carrying the source object, target object, mapping, scaling law or limit, initial values, retained historical information, error, and failure boundary together. It does not require all models to use the same representation, imply that every transfer across scales causes task-relevant loss, or identify losses from social recasting with physical entropy. Establishing a mathematical proof, showing that a real object meets its premises, and deciding which option to adopt in practice are three separate responsibilities.</p><p>Related studies are 05.05 “Gödel, Incompleteness, and Computability,” 05.06 “Effective Theories, Scale Closure, and Coarse-Graining,” and 05.08 “Model Selection, Parsimony, and Expressive Power.” These numbers help locate material. To check a particular mathematical conclusion, return to the assumptions, proofs, and domains of validity in the original works. A bibliography entry cannot substitute for verification.</p><p>See Appendix D for the framework’s map of applications by domain. Each domain retains its terminology, measurements, arguments, and professional boundaries. Teaching examples in the map illustrate conditions and the structure of counterexamples. They do not stand in for experiments that have been carried out or actual cases. Topic numbers connect existing research materials and do not rank theoretical importance.</p><h2 id="section-c-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>F1 Task Recovery and Worst-Case Error <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.6 F1 Task Recovery and Worst-Case Error {#section-c-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Let X be a nonempty set of source states, π:X→Y a deterministic representation with Y=π(X), and q:X→V the target. For each y∈Y, the preimage X_y={x:π(x)=y} is called a representation fiber. The decoder may use only y. It must not read the source state, identity labels, or undeclared history behind the scenes.</p><p><strong>Task recovery proposition.</strong> There exists g:Y→V such that q=g∘π if and only if q is constant on each fiber. If it exists, g is unique on the actual image Y.</p><p>Proof. If q=g∘π, the same representation is passed to the same function, so it gives the same target value. Conversely, on each nonempty fiber define its common target value to be g(y). This definition is unambiguous and gives g(π(x))=q(x) for every x. Each y has a source state, so any g satisfying the condition must take that common value. This proposition establishes only the existence of a function between sets. Whether it can be computed on an infinite domain by an algorithm that always halts, and whether a finite budget suffices, are separate questions.</p><p><strong>Finite scalar recovery proposition.</strong> Further suppose X is finite, q is real-valued, the decoder can output any real number, and loss is the worst-case absolute error over the entire domain. Let a_y=min_{x∈X_y}q(x) and b_y=max_{x∈X_y}q(x). Then</p><p>inf_g max_{x∈X}|q(x)−g(π(x))| = (1/2)max_{y∈Y}(b_y−a_y).</p><p>Proof. For any output c, the sum of its distances to the two endpoints is at least b_y−a_y. At least one distance is therefore at least half the range. With c=(a_y+b_y)/2, all target values in the fiber lie between the endpoints, and the maximum distance is exactly half the range. Taking the midpoint in each fiber and then the maximum over all fibers attains the lower bound.</p><p>For a nonnegative tolerance ε, a decoder with error at most ε over the entire domain exists if and only if the range of every fiber is at most 2ε. With an infinite state domain, use suprema and check boundedness. Vector values, restricted outputs, average loss, and probability weighting each give a different optimization problem. Half the range cannot serve as a universal score of information loss across domains.</p><p>Suppose the target is binary, the loss is zero-one loss, and the finite set of source states is uniformly distributed. Let n_y0 and n_y1 be the counts for the two labels in each fiber. The minimum error rate is |X|⁻¹∑_y min(n_y0,n_y1). Only one label can be chosen for each fiber. Choosing the more frequent label attains the smaller of the two error counts. The error under randomized labeling is a convex combination of these two counts and cannot be lower. This expression describes the given distribution. It does not estimate error frequencies in a real population.</p><h2 id="section-c-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>F4 Composition of Conversions and Actual Execution <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.7 F4 Composition of Conversions and Actual Execution {#section-c-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Let f:X→Z and h:Z→W, with the final query q already satisfying q=g∘f. The composition h∘f can still recover q if and only if g is constant on every fiber of h restricted to f(X). If q=k∘h∘f, intermediate values corresponding to the same final representation must have the same g value. Conversely, define k by the common g value on each final fiber. The condition concerns only the reachable image f(X). It imposes no constraint on intermediate values without a corresponding source state.</p><p>For example, let X={0,1}². The first stage retains both positions, and the second stores only their sum, while the final query still asks for the first position. The stages individually complete the tasks of storing and counting. Their composition merges (0,1) and (1,0), so it cannot answer the final query. Retaining the Boolean value at the first position repairs this problem without recovering all source details. Changing the final query to counting is simply a different task that can succeed.</p><p>Representational sufficiency and correct execution must also be tested separately. Suppose the representations of three source states are f=(0,0,1), the required answers are q=(0,1,1), and the actual rule is g_actual=(0,0). The first two states require different answers for representation 0, establishing that the representation is insufficient. The third state clearly requires 1 for representation 1, but the actual output is 0. This separately witnesses an independent error in the rule. Changing the rule to (0,1) repairs that error without removing the conflict in representation between the first two states. States for which the output is correct do not lose that correctness because of failures elsewhere.</p><p>This counterexample does not require every real-world fault to be uniquely classified. If the internal representation, input at the time, or actual rule is unknown, retain the gap. The continued existence of an original document cannot substitute for evidence that the reviewer obtained it in time. Being able to consult it after an error differs from having it available as input before the action.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.7"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>F2 Closure and F5 Error Propagation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.8 F2 Closure and F5 Error Propagation {#section-c-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Let F:X→X be deterministic one-step dynamics. There exists F̄:Y→Y satisfying π∘F=F̄∘π if and only if π(x)=π(x′) implies π(F(x))=π(F(x′)). The proof follows by replacing the target q in C.6 with π∘F. If the condition holds on the whole domain, then π∘F^t=F̄^t∘π for each nonnegative integer t. The zero-step case is immediate. To go from step t to step t+1, apply the commutation relation once.</p><p>If U is the set of permitted actions and each action has a specified F_u, verify the condition for each permitted action before composing the updates step by step for any action sequence fixed in advance. If action feasibility depends on states that were collapsed, also ensure that a macroscopic action can be executed in all relevant source states in the same fiber, or explicitly limit its availability. If different microscopic implementations are grouped under one macroscopic action, check separately whether they give the same macroscopic result.</p><p>In a finite stochastic system, let P(x,x′) be the transition kernel. If the probability sum ∑_{x′∈B}P(x,x′) for reaching each target block B is the same for every source state in a given block, define the macroscopic transition kernel using that common value. Whatever the conditional distribution of source states within the block given the current macroscopic block and past records, averaging that common value leaves it unchanged. The projected process therefore updates under this macroscopic kernel. If the same macroscopic one-step law is required for all initial distributions, the blockwise equality condition is also necessary. Check it by starting with all mass at each source state in the same block in turn. Retaining only the next-step mean, or obtaining equality by chance for one initial mixture, does not meet this distributional requirement for all initial states.</p><p>Approximate closure also needs a horizon. Let d be a metric on Y. Suppose each reachable x satisfies d(π(F(x)),F̄(π(x)))≤ε, and F̄ satisfies d(F̄(y),F̄(y′))≤L d(y,y′) in the region reached. Starting from y_0=π(x_0), let e_t=d(π(x_t),y_t). The triangle inequality gives e_{t+1}≤ε+Le_t, and the recurrence yields e_t≤ε∑_{j=0}^{t−1}L^j. If the per-step error or L changes over time, adapt the recurrence. The fixed-parameter formula must not still be applied.</p><p>These propositions state exact closure and error propagation under the conditions for approximate closure, respectively. They cannot determine whether an empirical model meets the statewise bound, whether it leaves the working region, or whether an intervention belongs to the original operation set. Closure under the usual distribution, closure under intervention, and normative usability still have to be established separately.</p><h2 id="section-c-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Different Tasks for Two Queues <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.9 Different Tasks for Two Queues {#section-c-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Consider two ordered queues (q₁,q₂)∈{0,…,30}², giving 961 states in total. The local overload target is max(q₁,q₂)&gt;15. For the sole purpose of computing expected error, make the 961 source states equiprobable. This differs from making the means equiprobable and does not imply that real queues follow a uniform distribution.</p><p>If only the mean m=(q₁+q₂)/2 is retained, (0,20) and (10,10) both give m=10, but the overload answers differ. For a sum s from 16 to 30, the same fiber has 2(s−15) overloaded states and 31−s non-overloaded states. The labels of the other fibers are unambiguous. The minimum number of classification errors is therefore ∑_{s=16}^{20}2(s−15)+∑_{s=21}^{30}(31−s)=85, and the error rate is 85/961≈8.844953%. This is the exact count on the complete artificial state domain.</p><p>The rule “report local overload only if the mean exceeds 15” misclassifies 240 states. The 155 errors above the optimal risk for the representation are due to the choice of rule. Those excess errors cannot all be attributed to information loss inherent in aggregation. If the task changes to recovering the maximum queue length, (0,30) and (15,15), both with mean 15, require answers of 30 and 15 respectively. The optimal real-valued output of 22.5 gives a worst-case error of 7.5. If the output must be an integer, the optimal radius for this fiber becomes 8. Output constraints themselves can therefore change the answer.</p><p>Adding the maximum M makes it possible to answer local overload and maximum length exactly, but still does not preserve which position is larger. With exactly two queues, the smaller value is 2m−M. If each position serves at most c tasks per round and no new tasks arrive, then</p><p>M′=(M−c)_+，m′=[(M−c)_++(2m−M−c)_+]/2,</p><p>where a_+=max(a,0). Thus (m,M) has dynamic closure under this symmetric model. When capacity is tied to position as (15,10), the states (0,20) and (20,0) with the same representation become (0,10) and (5,0) respectively. The next representations differ, so closure fails. With three queues, (20,20,5) and (20,15,10) both have mean 15 and maximum 20, but their totals after serving up to 15 queued tasks at each position are 10 and 5 respectively. The two-queue result cannot be extrapolated directly.</p><p>This example retains both positive and negative results. A total can answer a query about the total exactly, and adding a task-relevant variable can repair some shortcomings. The same addition need not work for interventions tied to position, heterogeneous dynamics, or more dimensions. Recovery of all microscopic information need not be a success condition for every task.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.9"}),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-c-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Strict Stability Condition for Feedback With Fixed Delay <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.10 The Strict Stability Condition for Feedback With Fixed Delay {#section-c-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Consider x_{t+1}=x_t−k x_{t−d}, where d is a fixed nonnegative integer, k is a fixed real number, and the initial history x_{−d},…,x_0 has finite values. The variable x is a signed deviation. The equation has no noise, external input, saturation, or state constraints. The zero solution is asymptotically stable for all initial histories if and only if</p><p>0&lt;k&lt;k_c(d)=2sin[π/(4d+2)].</p><p>Expanding the state to (x_t,x_{t−1},…,x_{t−d}) yields a finite-dimensional linear recurrence with characteristic polynomial p(λ,k)=λ^{d+1}−λ^d+k. All characteristic roots lie strictly inside the unit circle if and only if the solution tends to zero for every initial history. The modes associated with repeated roots decay despite their polynomial factors when the root modulus is strictly less than 1.</p><p>At k=0 there is a root at 1, so the system is not asymptotically stable. For k&lt;0, p(1,k)&lt;0, while p(λ,k) is positive for sufficiently large positive λ. There is therefore a real root greater than 1. For sufficiently small positive k, the root at 1 enters the unit circle as λ=1−k+O(k²). The remaining roots, originally at 0, stay inside the circle. Small positive gains are therefore stable. When d=0, the only root 1−k directly gives 0&lt;k&lt;2.</p><p>For positive k, write a boundary root as λ=e^{iθ}, with 0&lt;θ&lt;2π. From k=λ^d(1−λ) we obtain</p><p>k=2sin(θ/2)exp{i[(d+1/2)θ−π/2]}.</p><p>For k to be positive and real, θ=(π+4πm)/(2d+1) with m=0,…,d. The first boundary at positive gain comes from the smallest angle and its conjugate angle, giving k_c(d). The roots vary continuously with the parameter and are bounded at finite parameter values. They must pass through the boundary before leaving the unit circle.</p><p>We must also rule out renewed stability at higher gains. On the unit circle, p_λ=λ^{d−1}[(d+1)λ−d] is nonzero, so boundary roots are simple. Implicit differentiation gives</p><p>d log|λ|/dk = (2d+1)(1−cosθ)/{k|(d+1)e^{iθ}−d|²}&gt;0.</p><p>Thus all crossings at positive k go outward, with no re-entry. Together with stability at small gains, this gives the necessary and sufficient interval stated above. Equality at the boundary leaves a nondecaying mode. A root modulus slightly below 1 in floating-point arithmetic is no basis for including the boundary. Above the boundary a growing mode exists, while the special all-zero history still remains zero.</p><p>With k=0.8 fixed, the critical values for d=0 and 1 are 2 and 1, respectively, and the system is stable. The critical values for d=2 and 3 are approximately 0.618034 and 0.445042, respectively, and the system is unstable. For an all-ones initial history, the first step in all four cases gives 0.2. The feedback term already affects the state in the model, while convergence differs. Even when the system is stable, the result still places no bound on transient peaks or costs and does not establish acceptability to people or real-world repair. Time-varying delays, stochastic disturbances, nonlinearity, and constraints on execution require new analysis.</p><h2 id="section-c-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Historical Increments and Omissions in the Current Representation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.11 Historical Increments and Omissions in the Current Representation {#section-c-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use the same 961 equiprobable initial states, but let each queue serve at most 5 tasks per round with no new arrivals. Advance the full state by F(q₁,q₂)=((q₁−5)_+,(q₂−5)_+). The prediction target is the mean after the second round. Compare four inputs after the first round: the current mean, the current mean with the initial mean, the full current state, and the full current state with the initial mean. Under squared loss, outputting the conditional mean of the target for each input fiber attains the minimum expected loss.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Prediction Input</th><th style="text-align:right;">Minimum Mean Squared Error Under the Given Distribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Current mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.579016561</td></tr><tr><td>Current mean and initial mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.271143397</td></tr><tr><td>Full current state of both queues</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr><tr><td>Full current state of both queues and initial mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The calculation advances each of the 961 initial states and retains its original weight. It does not make the states after the first round uniform again. In particular, (0,20) advances to (0,15) and has a next mean of 5. The state (10,15) advances to (5,10) and has a next mean of 2.5. Both current means are 7.5, while the initial means differ. The historical mean helps distinguish differences collapsed by the current projection. The full current state already suffices to determine the future.</p><p>This establishes that a historical predictive increment can arise from an omission in the current representation. It does not establish that real systems universally lack memory. Real data still require attention to measurement, confounding, temporal order, leakage, and model selection. If the full current carrier is unavailable, one cannot claim to have ruled out a historical increment. If earlier experience affects the outcome through a current skill and the residual disappears once that skill is represented sufficiently, the result limits only the additional prediction given the skill. It does not negate the total effect of earlier experience or rule out learning in general.</p><h2 id="section-c-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">C.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Three Limits on Composing Local Successes <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-c-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;C.12 Three Limits on Composing Local Successes {#section-c-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Whether conversions can compose to give the same final answer, whether causal effects can link into a chain, and whether actions can be jointly executed with available resources concern functions, joint responses, and sets of constraints, respectively. They can occur in the same workflow. A generic status of “connected” cannot make them interchangeable.</p><p>See §2.4.2 for the causal counterexample. The types s∈{0,1} have equal weight, with m(a)=a(1−s) and y(a,m)=sm. The average effect from treatment to mediator and the average effect from mediator to outcome are both 1/2, whereas the effect of the full chain is 0 because different types carry the two effects. This rules out multiplying edge means unconditionally. It does not withdraw an independently identified total effect.</p><p>The resource counterexample consists of two unit tasks, each taking a full period. Both must be completed before the end of the first period, which has capacity of only 1. Each task is feasible alone, but there is no feasible joint schedule. Joint execution becomes feasible if capacity in that period rises to 2, or if one deadline is legitimately moved to the second period and that period has capacity of 1. If both original deadlines remain, adding capacity in the second period cannot make up for the original deadline. The conclusion follows directly from the stated constraints. It does not certify real-world demand, capacity, or authorization.</p><p><strong>F3 Common feasible action proposition.</strong> For a finite, single-round deterministic task, let X_y be a nonempty observation class and A_ok(x) the set of eligible actions for each state. There exists an action depending only on y that is eligible for every x∈X_y if and only if ∩_{x∈X_y}A_ok(x) is nonempty.</p><p>Proof. If such an action a exists, it belongs to the eligible set of every compatible state and therefore to the common intersection. Conversely, choose any a in the nonempty intersection. It is eligible in every compatible state, and choosing it does not require knowing which state actually holds. Nonemptiness of each state’s set separately is only a necessary condition. For example, if the eligible sets of two states are {左} and {右}, meaning left and right, respectively, each has an option but they share no action. If both allow an authorized preservation action, the intersection can be nonempty while the state remains incompletely identified.</p><p>The eligible sets in the proposition must already incorporate the resources, protections, and valid permissions required by the task. Writing an action in mathematical terms cannot substitute for these real-world conditions. The proposition concerns only the listed compatible states and cannot guarantee that unknown states will never occur. When undertaken lawfully, further observation, waiting, or preservation can change the information and policy sets. None of these occurs automatically. Probability-based choices that tolerate risk, randomization, and sequential policies are analyzed separately according to their own objectives. Representational sufficiency, computability, accessibility, implementability, resources, and authority to adopt must be justified separately for the specific use.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"C.12"}),e[3]||(e[3]=l("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const g=n(c,[["render",h]]);export{b as __pageData,g as default};
